<h1>Vocab: Inverted-Hull Outlines</h1>
<dl>
<dt>inverted hull</dt>
<dd>An enlarged copy of an object drawn in the outline color with front
faces culled, so only a silhouette-hugging rim of it survives behind the
real object. The zero-shader cartoon outline.</dd>

<dt>face culling</dt>
<dd>Skipping triangles by winding order. BACK culling (normal) hides the far
side; FRONT culling (<code>rlSetCullFace(RL_CULL_FACE_FRONT)</code>) hides
the near side — the hull trick's key ingredient.</dd>

<dt>render batch</dt>
<dd>rlgl's accumulation buffer for immediate-mode shapes: draw calls queue
geometry, the GPU sees it at flush time. GL state changes bind at the FLUSH,
not the call.</dd>

<dt>batch drain</dt>
<dd><code>rlDrawRenderBatchActive()</code> — an explicit flush. Mandatory
before any state change (cull, blend) that must not leak onto geometry
already queued.</dd>

<dt>hull scale</dt>
<dd>The inflation factor (ours: 1.06 about each part's center). Sets rim
thickness; uniform because every character is contract-scaled to the same
1.8u height.</dd>

<dt>grouped passes</dt>
<dd>Outline pass for ALL parts, then color pass for all parts — two drains
per character instead of two per part. Never interleave state switches per
primitive in a batched renderer.</dd>

<dt>corner gap</dt>
<dd>The hull failure mode on hard edges: inflated flat faces separate at
sharp corners, opening holes in the rim. Mitigations: small scale,
per-recipe opt-out, or (elsewhere) post-process outlines.</dd>

<dt>silhouette rim</dt>
<dd>The visible band where the hull extends past the real object on screen —
the outline itself, which follows the object's edge from every camera angle
without any per-view work.</dd>
</dl>
